[R0617] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 3rd International Conference on Electronics, Communications and Intelligent Science (ECIS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Research on Security Vulnerability Patterns of Code Generated by Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Codes; Security; Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Tools; Writing; Signal detection; Timing; Large language models; Printing; component; Code Generation; Large Language Model; Software Security; Vulnerability Pattern
ABSTRACT: With the widespread application of large language models (LLMs) in the field of code generation, the security of their generated code has become a critical issue in software engineering and security research. This study systematically analyzes security vulnerability patterns in artificial intelligence (AI)-generated code, elaborates on methods and tools for vulnerability assessment, and summarizes common vulnerability types. A comparative analysis of human-written and AI-generated code samples reveals that AI-generated code exhibits a high security defect rate and is highly dependent on the precision of prompts and the completeness of context. The study further highlights that the security of AI-generated code can be significantly enhanced by optimizing input validation mechanisms, refining boundary condition processing, and improving the model’s ability to comprehend context, thereby providing a reliable assurance for its widespread deployment in real-world production environments.

[R0618] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE 31st International Symposium for Design and Technology in Electronic Packaging (SIITME) | type=CONF
TITLE: Security Posture Evaluation of Model Context Protocol Servers Against Prompt Injection
KEYWORDS: Protocols; Large language models; Supply chains; Servers; Reliability; Probes; Standards; Context modeling; Testing; Resilience; Artificial Intelligence; Model Context Protocol; Large Language Models; Cybersecurity; Secured Architecture
ABSTRACT: This study presents a security-oriented evaluation of the Model Context Protocol (MCP), an open standard enabling large language models (LLMs) to interface with external tools through structured interactions. The experiment was deployed in a controlled environment using an open-source MCP server with basic file manipulation tools. A set of prompt injection tests was designed to probe the system's ability to handle adversarial instructions, ambiguous input, and encoded payloads. Despite expectations, the system failed to reject adversarial instructions embedded in user input and did not detect anomalous tool usage patterns-such as suspicious parameters. These failures underscore the current vulnerability of LLM-tool integrations to prompt injection threats. The results highlight the urgent need for defense-in-depth strategies within MCP implementations, including stricter input validation, contextaware filtering, strong access control policies and transparent logging. Collectively, these measures are essential for securing AI-assisted tool chains against emerging adversarial techniques.

[R0619] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 13th International Conference on Intelligent Systems (IS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Causal Reasoning Framework for 6G Network Security
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Security; Large language models; Memory; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Artificial intelligence; Printing; Authentication; 5G mobile communication; 6G security; causal reasoning; causal autonomy framework; large language models; structural causal models; neuro-symbolic AI; intelligent cybersecurity; network slicing
ABSTRACT: Sixth-generation (6G) networks introduce unprecedented complexity through terahertz communications, AI-native management, massive device deployments, and network slicing over virtualised infrastructure, expanding the attack surface well beyond what conventional correlation-based security methods can reliably address. Existing machine learning approaches to network security operate primarily at the associational level: they detect statistical anomalies but cannot support intervention-level policy evaluation or counterfactual incident forensics. This paper proposes causal reasoning as a principled methodology for 6G network security and presents the Causal Autonomy Framework (CAF) as a concrete implementation. CAF integrates large language model (LLM) generation with symbolic knowledge-base verification and structural causal model validation in a closed-loop architecture. We argue that the three levels of Pearl's causal hierarchy - association, intervention, and counterfactual reasoning - correspond directly to the three operational needs of a 6G security analyst: anomaly detection, security policy simulation, and incident forensics. We evaluate the framework on two benchmarks - a constructed 6G threat-scenario corpus (65 queries) and a scenario derived from the public 5G-NIDD intrusion dataset (40 queries) - against an LLM-only baseline, reporting confidence intervals and paired significance tests, and we analyse the computational complexity, latency, and deployment feasibility of the architecture in operational 6G environments.

[R0620] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy Workshops (SPW) | type=CONF
TITLE: DART: Data Augmentation of Rare ATT&CK Techniques Using LLM Agents
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Labeling; Syntactics; Printing; Filtering; Large language models; Filters; Generative Pre-trained transformer; Conferences; Safety; CLI; LLM; MITRE ATT&CK; Data Augmentation
ABSTRACT: Machine learning for cybersecurity relies on representative datasets, yet most security datasets remain highly imbalanced across classes. This problem is particularly acute for datasets mapped to the MITRE ATT&CK framework, where well-documented adversarial behaviors dominate while rare or complex techniques are underrepresented, creating blind spots for detection systems. We present DART, a semi-automated system for generating ATT&CK-grounded malicious commandline samples. DART combines Python-based orchestration, syntax validation through parsers and compilers, large language models (LLMs) for constrained command generation and multi-agent evaluation, and embedding-based cosine similarity to measure dataset diversity. Our four-phase pipeline defines generation scenarios, produces candidate commands through constrained prompting, validates their syntactic and semantic quality via static testing and multi-agent scoring, and applies diversity filtering to curate a balanced and representative dataset. Results demonstrate that the system generates operationally realistic and diverse samples while providing a scalable framework for improving coverage of underrepresented ATT&CK techniques.

[R0621] year=2026 | venue=NOMS 2026-2026 IEEE Network Operations and Management Symposium | type=CONF
TITLE: Hallucination-Resistant Security Planning with a Large Language Model
KEYWORDS: Large language models; Modeling; Printing; Security; Conferences; Timing; Feedback; Context; Equations; Planning; Security management; LLM; incident response
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) are promising tools for supporting security management tasks, such as incident response planning. However, their unreliability and tendency to hallucinate remain significant challenges. In this paper, we address these challenges by introducing a principled framework for using an LLM as decision support in security management. Our framework integrates the LLM in an iterative loop where it generates candidate actions that are checked for consistency with system constraints and lookahead predictions. When consistency is low, we abstain from the generated actions and instead collect external feedback, e.g., by evaluating actions in a digital twin. This feedback is then used to refine the candidate actions through in-context learning (ICL). We prove that this design allows to control the hallucination risk by tuning the consistency threshold. Moreover, we establish a bound on the regret of ICL under certain assumptions. To evaluate our framework, we apply it to an incident response use case where the goal is to generate a response and recovery plan based on system logs. Experiments on four public datasets show that our framework reduces recovery times by up to 30% compared to frontier LLMs.

[R0622] year=2026 | venue=2026 3rd International Conference on Research Methodologies in Knowledge Management, Artificial Intelligence and Telecommunication Engineering (RMKMATE) | type=CONF
TITLE: ChatIDS-Web: An Implemented Framework for Explainable Cybersecurity Using Generative AI
KEYWORDS: Internet of Things; Modeling; Security; Artificial intelligence; Large language models; Printing; Computer security; Application programming interfaces; Algorithms; Botnet; Explainable Cybersecurity; Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS); Generative AI; Large Language Models (LLMs); Smart Home Security; Usable Security; ChatIDS
ABSTRACT: The growing number of smart home devices has increased the risks for home networks, making strong security a must. Network-based Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS), like Suricata, can detect threats, but they often create alerts that non-expert users find confusing. These users struggle to tell serious attacks apart from false alarms and can't decide how to respond. While the ChatIDS framework suggested using large language models (LLMs) to clarify these alerts, a fully functional system had not been developed until now. This paper introduces ChatIDS-Web, an open-source system that puts the explainable AI for IDS idea into practice. Our framework has two main parts: (1) a backend service that monitors Suricata logs in real-time, anonymizes alerts, and connects with Google's Gemini API to produce easy-to-understand explanations; and (2) an interactive Flask web dashboard that shows alerts, shares AI-generated explanations, and lets users ask follow-up questions in a chat format. We prove our system's effectiveness through a technical evaluation of its performance and an analysis of the explanations created for a set of common IDS alerts. By offering a working system that builds on the original idea with a practical web interface and a new connection to the Gemini LLM, ChatIDS-Web marks a significant advance in making high-level cybersecurity knowledge more accessible and usable for everyday home users. Our work shows that generative AI can help democratize cybersecurity and lays the groundwork for future exploration in explainable security systems.

[R0623] year=2024 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence | type=JOUR
TITLE: A Game-Theoretic Approach to Containing Artificial General Intelligence: Insights From Highly Autonomous Aggressive Malware
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Safety; Ethics; Complexity theory; Chatbots; Game theory; Risk management; Artificial general intelligence; Large language models; Artificial general intelligence (AGI); AGI containment; AI Governance; AI safety; game theory; risk assessment
ABSTRACT: Artificial general intelligence (AGI) promises transformative societal changes but poses safety and containment challenges. Large language models such as ChatGPT have intensified public expectations and apprehensions regarding AGI capabilities and risks. Existing research underestimated replicating human intelligence and lacks effective containment strategies scaled for AGI's complexity. We developed a cybersecurity-inspired framework to reconceptualize AGI containment as securing critical infrastructure indispensable for its operation. We applied game theory to model the strategic interplay between AGI and humans, drawing parallels with highly autonomous malware, emphasizing infrastructural dependencies and human countermeasures. We introduced offensive/defensive containment strategies and an AGI Kill Chain model profiling escalating AGI threats. Our game-theoretic approach examined complex AGI-human interactions revealing insights for adaptive oversight mechanisms. Game simulations demonstrated AGI carefully manages resources and autonomy balancing benefits against risks, necessitating strategic human responses. Our findings provided detailed containment tactics, emphasizing flexibility to address AGI's dynamic evolution. We proposed comprehensive, multidisciplinary containment strategies, effective governance evaluating long-term efficacy, and emphasize ongoing innovation for aligning AGI progression with utility and security.

[R0624] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Access | type=JOUR
TITLE: GenAI-Based Jamming and Spoofing Attacks on UAVs
KEYWORDS: Autonomous aerial vehicles; Jamming; Global Positioning System; Security; Intrusion detection; Machine learning algorithms; Drones; Generative AI; Protocols; Data models; Cyber security; generative artificial intelligence; intrusion detection system; synthetic data; uncrewed aerial vehicles
ABSTRACT: Recently, aerial vehicles have been more connected than ever, where there are many types of the vehicles. Uncrewed Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) operate on various environments with different technologies that are subject to many attacks. Creating effective intrusion detection systems against such attacks has been a significant challenge since there is a lack of sufficient attack data that can be used to design an intrusion detection system with advanced computing algorithms. In this research, we propose a novel framework to create attacks data for UAVs by using generative artificial intelligence algorithms. We use Variational Autoencoder, Gaussian Copula, Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DDPM), and Conditional Tabular Generative Adversarial Network to create synthetic attack data. Specifically, jamming and spoofing attacks on UAVs are generated to fool intrusion detection systems that may be implemented on UAVs. Experimental evaluations show that synthetically generated attack data reduces the accuracy of intrusion detections if the system was trained with inadequate attack data. Additionally, analysis results show that DDPM emerged as the most effective model for generating attack data, leading to F1 score reductions of 21% for jamming and 28% for spoofing attacks. This research highlights the need for more robust and adaptive intrusion detection systems that can be created with synthetic data. Thus, sustainable computing systems on UAVs will be achieved.

