[R0626] year=2026 | venue=ICASSP 2026 - 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) | type=CONF
TITLE: ThreatSage: A Modular Benchmark for LLM-Orchestrated Blue Team Defense Operations
KEYWORDS: Telemetry; Aerospace and electronic systems; Feeds; Protocols; Communication systems; IP networks; Computer networks; Telecommunications; HTTP; Data communication; Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; Threat Intelligence
ABSTRACT: As cyber threats grow in complexity, blue team defenders require robust systems for multi-stage analysis. We introduce ThreatSage, a modular benchmark for evaluating Large Language Models (LLMs) in orchestrated blue team defense operations. ThreatSage defines a structured function-calling pipeline across four stages: threat attribution, behavioral analysis, prioritization, and mitigation. Each stage integrates domain-specific functions such as entity extraction and response generation. The benchmark comprises 30 task types and over 450,000 instances sourced from 23 threat intelligence datasets. Experiments across leading LLMs show that ThreatSage improves precision, task dependency handling, and robustness against noise, outperforming prompting-based methods like Chain-of-Thought. ThreatSage establishes a realistic framework for secure, autonomous threat reasoning in operational environments.

[R0627] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Data Science for Cyber-Physical Systems Resilience using Advanced Applications (ICDCA) | type=CONF
TITLE: Neural Synthesis of Perimeter Defense Policies: Fine-Tuning Language Models for Automated Intrusion Countermeasure Generation
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Security; Printing; Tuning; Syntactics; Training; Conferences; Computer security; Artificial intelligence; Machine learning; Firewalls; Intrusion Detection Systems; Large Language Models; Fine-tuning; Cybersecurity Automation; Rule Generation
ABSTRACT: Network security administrators face a critical expertise gap: configuring intrusion detection systems and firewall policies requires specialized knowledge of both attack patterns and rule syntax. This paper investigates the efficacy of finetuned large language models in automating the generation of defensive security rules. We systematically evaluate baseline capabilities of instruction-tuned models across thirty distinct cyberattack scenarios, encompassing both traffic filtering and payload inspection rule sets. Three progressive fine-tuning strategies are implemented to enhance model comprehension of security semantics and rule syntax. Experimental results demonstrate substantial performance improvements, with fine-tuned models achieving 89% accuracy in generating syntactically correct and technically effective countermeasures, compared to 42% baseline performance. The study provides empirical evidence that domainspecific fine-tuning bridges the cybersecurity knowledge gap while maintaining rule efficiency and minimizing false positive potential.

[R0628] year=2025 | venue=2025 8th International Balkan Conference on Communications and Networking (Balkancom) | type=CONF
TITLE: Harnessing Large Language Models for Passive SCADA Security Risk Assessment: A Case Study
KEYWORDS: Training; Protocols; Large language models; Systems operation; SCADA systems; Telecommunication traffic; Real-time systems; Risk management; Standards; Resilience; SCADA; LLM; passive analysis; S7comm; cyber security
ABSTRACT: Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) systems play a vital role in critical infrastructure, but outdated protocols and delicate operational requirements undermine their cybersecurity. Active scanning poses a risk of disruption, which drives the need for passive methods. This paper investigates using three large language models (LLMs) to assess SCADA risks by analyzing Wireshark captures of network traffic without interfering with system operations. Tested on a Siemens S7 -1500 PLC scenario, the proposed framework processes traffic data and produces risk reports nonintrusively. The framework ef-fectively identifies vulnerabilities, assesses protocol-specific risks, and generates structured risk reports as an alternative to active vulnerability scanning. Per IEC 62443 standards, this method strengthens SCADA security while maintaining operational con-tinuity.

[R0629] year=2026 | venue=2026 2nd International Conference on Smart Technologies for Sustainable Development Goals (ICSTSDG) | type=CONF
TITLE: Intelligent Intrusion Detection and Prevention Systems: The Role of AI and Machine Learning
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Artificial intelligence; Intrusion detection; Signal detection; Machine learning; Internet of Things; Timing; Real-time systems; Printing; Learning (artificial intelligence); Intrusion Detection; Artificial Intelligence; Deep Learning; Cybersecurity; Anomaly Detection; Machine Learning
ABSTRACT: The growing complexity and frequency of cyberattacks have demonstrated the weaknesses of conventional IDPS, which are highly dependent on fixed rules and signature-based approaches. Artificial Intelligence (AI) has become a transformative solution to overcome these difficulties, allowing intrusion detection to be more adaptive, scalable and intelligent. This research paper discusses AI-driven IDPS through the lens of ML, DL, and RL models to detect and prevent network and host-based threats. The paper presents major datasets, evaluation metrics, and instruments that are typically used in research, as well as major limitations including data imbalance, adversarial attacks, model interpretability, and real-time processing constraints. Moreover, the paper talks about integration into modern security ecosystems, such as cloud, IoT, and edge environment. The future directions are described, with the focus on such developments as autonomous self-learning systems, federated learning, LLM, and edge-native detection mechanisms. The results show that AI-based IDPS provide a great opportunity to improve the resilience of cybersecurity, but additional studies are needed to eliminate current constraints and implement powerful, reliable, and scalable solutions.

[R0630] year=2024 | venue=2024 International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Next Generation Networks (ICNGN) | type=CONF
TITLE: Large Language Model-enabled Vulnerability Investigation: A Review
KEYWORDS: Ethics; Accuracy; Reviews; Computational modeling; Scalability; Prevention and mitigation; Large language models; Robustness; Computer security; Next generation networking; Large Language Models; Vulnerability Discovery; Vulnerability Validation; Vulnerability Exploitation
ABSTRACT: In recent years, the integration of large language models (LLMs) into cybersecurity has demonstrated significant potential in enhancing vulnerability analysis. This paper provides a comprehensive review of current literature, focusing on the applications of LLMs in vulnerability discovery, exploitation, and validation. We examine various LLM-powered frameworks that have automated aspects of vulnerability analysis, reduced the time required for vulnerability identification, and improved the precision of vulnerability assessment. In addition, we discuss LLM-driven advancements in security vulnerability exploitation and validation, which facilitate more efficient and accurate mitigation. The contributions of this review include an extensive synthesis of existing studies, a proposed framework that high-lights the role of LLMs across different stages of the vulnerability lifecycle, and an outline of future research directions in LLM-based cybersecurity. Our findings aim to guide researchers and practitioners in developing robust, scalable, and automated cybersecurity solutions powered by LLMs.

[R0631] year=2026 | venue=ICASSP 2026 - 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP) | type=CONF
TITLE: From Intent to Invocation: A Reasoning-First Framework for Natural Language to Penetration Testing Commands
KEYWORDS: Filtering; Feedback; Filters; Circuits; Protocols; Computer networks; LoRa; Cyberspace; Communication systems; Communication networks; Command Generation; Penetration Testing
ABSTRACT: The automated translation of natural language into penetration testing commands represents a critical challenge in cybersecurity. Although Large Language Models demonstrate considerable potential in this domain, they frequently generate syntactically invalid commands or hallucinate non-existent parameters. To address these limitations, we propose Penetron, a framework that enhances command generation accuracy through a novel two-stage training paradigm. In the first stage, supervised fine-tuning is performed on a curated Chain-of-Thought dataset to cultivate fundamental reasoning capabilities. In the second stage, the model is further optimized via reinforcement learning with Generative Reward Policy Optimization, guided by a composite reward function that jointly evaluates structural validity and semantic correctness. To facilitate systematic evaluation, we introduce NL2PEN-KALI, a benchmark specifically designed for penetration testing scenarios. Experimental results show that Penetron achieves substantial improvements over state-of-the-art models on both NL2PEN-KALI and the public NL2SH-ALFA benchmark, producing commands with higher syntactic validity and contextual relevance.

[R0632] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy Workshops (SPW) | type=CONF
TITLE: Limitations of Biological Risk Evaluation for Llms and Autonomous Science
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Printing; Large language models; Indexes; Indexing; Displays; Artificial intelligence; Testing; Laboratories; Current
ABSTRACT: Large language models are increasingly used to accelerate scientific research and solve critical societal challenges. However, there are increasing concerns that these models raise biodefense, biosecurity, and biosafety risks since they can be misused or may themselves conduct harmful actions. Methodologies such as benchmarking and red-teaming are used to evaluate models; however, these approaches have limitations when they are applied to assessing a model's biological risk. In this work, we outline the challenges current methods face in evaluating the biological risk posed by LLMs, agentic systems, and autonomous systems as well as possible mitigations for these challenges.

[R0633] year=2024 | venue=2024 IEEE International Conference on Cyber Security and Resilience (CSR) | type=CONF
TITLE: CyberMetric: A Benchmark Dataset based on Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Evaluating LLMs in Cybersecurity Knowledge
KEYWORDS: Accuracy; Reverse engineering; Benchmark testing; NIST Standards; Risk management; Problem-solving; Computer security
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used across various domains, from software development to cyber threat intelligence. Understanding all the different cybersecurity fields, including topics such as cryptography, reverse engineering, and risk assessment, poses a challenge even for human experts. The research community needs a diverse, accurate, and up-to-date dataset to test the general knowledge of LLMs in cybersecurity. To address this gap, we present CyberMetric-80, CyberMetric-500, CyberMetric-2000, and CyberMetric-10000, which are multiple-choice Q&A benchmark datasets comprising 80, 500, 2000, and 10,000 questions, respectively. By utilizing GPT-3.5 and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), we collected documents, including NIST standards, research papers, publicly accessible books, RFCs, and other publications in the cybersecurity domain, to generate questions, each with four possible answers. The results underwent several rounds of error checking and refinement. Human experts invested over 200 hours validating the questions and solutions to ensure their accuracy and relevance and to filter out any questions unrelated to cybersecurity. We have evaluated and compared 25 state-of-the-art LLM models on the CyberMetric datasets. In addition to our primary goal of evaluating LLMs, we involved 30 human participants to solve CyberMetric-80 in a closed-book scenario. The results can serve as a reference for comparing the general cybersecurity knowledge of humans and LLMs. The findings revealed that GPT-4o, GPT-4-turbo, Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct, Falcon-180B-Chat, and GEMINI-pro 1.0 were the best-performing LLMs. Additionally, the top LLMs were more accurate than humans on CyberMetric-80, although highly experienced human experts still outperformed small models such as Llama-3-8B, Phi-2 or Gemma-7b. The CyberMetric dataset is publicly available for the research community and can be downloaded from the projects' website: https://github.com/CyberMetric.

[R0634] year=2024 | venue=2024 IEEE International Conference on Cyber Security and Resilience (CSR) | type=CONF
TITLE: A Modular Generative Honeypot Shell
KEYWORDS: Prevention and mitigation; Large language models; Computer architecture; Oral communication; Software; Software measurement; Prompt engineering; honeypot; shell; generative artificial intelligence; large language models
ABSTRACT: In this work, we present Limbosh, a generative honeypot shell written in Python that places attackers in a conversation with a large language model (LLM) configured to behave like a real shell. The use of generative AI in place of traditional honeypot shell software admits the development of arbitrary honeypot configurations by adjusting the prompt used to seed the LLM context. Key features of Limbosh include: a flexible prompt generation system based on text templating and reusable prompt fragments; the ability to make use of arbi-trary LLMs; sophisticated prompt injection mitigation measures; and a highly modular and configurable architecture permitting straightforward expansion of its feature set and enhancement of its capabilities. To demonstrate its utility and practicality, we ran a single-blind, within-subjects study of the interaction of four cybersecurity professionals with Limbosh compared to a control shell. We find that Limbosh is capable of convincingly emulating real shell software, even when faced with professional users. We present our experimental results, and make the Limbosh software itself open-source and freely available.

[R0635] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Consumer Electronics | type=JOUR
TITLE: GenFed-IDS: A Lightweight Federated Generative AI Framework for UAV Anomaly Detection in Rescue Operations
KEYWORDS: Autonomous aerial vehicles; Robustness; Accuracy; Collaboration; Anomaly detection; Security; Consumer electronics; Computational modeling; Generative adversarial networks; Federated learning; IDS; UAV; generative AI; federated learning; variational autoencoder; cyber-physical security
ABSTRACT: Unmanned Aerial Vehicles are increasingly deployed in consumer applications such as logistics, disaster recovery, and surveillance, yet their wireless communication links remain highly vulnerable to cyber-attacks. Traditional intrusion detection systems (IDS) often struggle in UAV environments due to resource constraints, dynamic network conditions, and scarcity of labeled datasets. To address these challenges, we propose a Generative AI-enabled lightweight IDS framework tailored for UAV communication networks. The framework integrates hybrid Convolutional Neural Network–Gated Recurrent Unit (CNN-GRU) autoencoders with generative augmentation and knowledge distillation, achieving high accuracy while maintaining computational efficiency. Explainability is incorporated via SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis to ensure trustworthy and interpretable decision-making. Experimental evaluations on a multimodal UAV dataset demonstrate state-of-the-art performance with 99.49% accuracy, 99.48% recall, and AUROC of 0.9999, alongside a student model that reduces model size, inference latency, and memory footprint by nearly 50%. Comparative results confirm that the proposed framework outperforms recent IDS baselines in both detection capability and lightweight deployment, offering a practical and scalable solution for next-generation UAV communication networks.

[R0636] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Open Journal of the Computer Society | type=JOUR
TITLE: Generative AI for Ransomware Identification and Mitigation: Taxonomy, Challenges, and Future Directions
KEYWORDS: Payloads; Military aircraft; Space technology; Telemetry; Aerospace and electronic systems; Radio broadcasting; Frequency modulation; Central Processing Unit; Integrated circuits; System-on-chip; Generative AI; ransomware; LLM; GAN; detection; mitigation
ABSTRACT: Ransomware is a relevant threat that is causing losses in millions and affecting several organizations worldwide. Emerging actors leverage strategies such as living-off-the-land, obfuscation, and fileless malware to perform increasingly sophisticated attacks. Generative AI is now being considered as a tool for combating cyberattacks, but its real-world effectiveness, risks, and deployment trade-offs are still unclear in the field of ransomware, pointing to the need for a thorough, rigorous review. This review paper investigates the application of generative AI techniques for the detection and mitigation of ransomware attacks. To this end, we introduce a taxonomy for categorizing the solutions presented in recent studies, thereby offering deeper insights, detecting challenges, and proposing research directions. We also discuss misalignment between academia and industry solutions given the current ransomware landscape. This research presents a first analysis of transformer models and generative adversarial networks leveraged exclusively to detect and mitigate ransomware. Our review reveals that there is a need for dedicated and up-to-date ransomware datasets, new detection strategies, and new data for static and dynamic analysis.

[R0637] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 31st International Conference on Emerging Technologies and Factory Automation (ETFA) | type=CONF
TITLE: Toward Reliable Industrial CVE Profiling with LLMs: Comparing Prompt-Based and Workflow-Based Guardrails
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Large language models; Grounding; Runtime; Design methodology; Radio access networks; Regional area networks; Measurement; Printing; Reflection; CVE profiling; industrial cybersecurity; large language models; agentic systems; workflow guardrails; evidence grounding
ABSTRACT: OT environments keep industrial assets in service far longer than typical IT systems, which increases cybersecurity exposure and forces remediation decisions to rely on evidence scattered across vendor bulletins, advisories, and release notes. Assembling this evidence into a coherent profile, a task referred to as CVE profiling, requires matching affected versions, identifying exploit preconditions, and reconciling remediation guidance across sources that differ in scope and authority. Applying off-the-shelf LLM-based deep-research systems is a natural starting point, but their outputs are often unreliable. In this work, we identify five core reliability challenges and design guardrails to address them in two LLM-based variants: a prompt-controlled agent (soft approach), which encodes the discipline through instructions, and a workflow with runtime controls (hard approach), which enforces it through explicit gates. Evaluated on a 24-case industrial benchmark spanning controllers, supervisory software, networking devices, and cross-cutting component vulnerabilities, both variants outperform a generic baseline across all metrics. Beyond the overall gains, each variant shows strengths in distinct quality dimensions. The soft variant leads on profile completeness and factual quality, improving Coverage and Factualness by 25% and 20% over the baseline, while hard variant leads on evidence grounding, raising Grounded Correctness by 36%. Together, these results show that structured guardrails can make LLM-based deep research substantially more reliable for vulnerability management, and that soft variant can serve as a competitive, lightweight alternative to workflow enforcement when grounding requirements are moderate.

[R0638] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE 7th International Conference on Cognitive Machine Intelligence (CogMI) | type=CONF
TITLE: Automated Threat Emulation Framework with LLMs for Cyber Physical Systems
KEYWORDS: Substation automation; Systematics; Subject matter experts; Emulation; Decision making; Strategic planning; Real-time systems; Threat assessment; Robustness; Smart grids; Cyber Physical Systems (CPS); MITRE ATT&CK; Threat emulation; Substation Automation System; Causal Loop Diagram (CLD); Large Language Model (LLM); AI planning; Machine Learning (ML); Smart grids
ABSTRACT: Securing cyber-physical systems (CPS), which often form a nation’s critical infrastructure, is becoming increasingly challenging. New threats are arising due to vulnerabilities in the Information Technology (IT), Operational Technology (OT), and their interactions. Moreover, the growth in artificial intelligence (AI) technologies have made it easy for attackers to exploit system vulnerabilities. A primary concern is the insufficient effectiveness of cyber operations and automated decision-making processes, which currently rely heavily on manual evaluation and subject matter experts (SMEs). A primary concern is the limited effectiveness of security evaluation methodologies in understanding a system’s behavior during an attack. Current methodologies are generally suited for IT environments and do not easily adapt to OT due to their complexity. In addition, cybersecurity evaluation methods in OT often depend on manual evaluation and subject matter experts (SMEs).This paper introduces a novel framework based on LLMs and AI planning to automate threat emulation, facilitating the evaluation of the cybersecurity posture of OT systems. The newly introduced ‘Automated Threat Emulation Framework’ integrates with MITRE ATT&CK for automatic execution of various Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) employed by the real-time attacks for emulating the threats. Our framework employs multiple specialized LLM-driven agents that collaboratively perform tasks of context summarization, strategic planning, decision-making, external data retrieval, automated code generation, and secure execution. Central to our methodology is the incorporation of AI Planning and Causal Loop Diagrams (CLD) to accurately simulate cyber-attacks and systematically analyze cascading effects across interconnected CPS components. Through a comprehensive prototype implementation and a detailed case study involving a digital substation automation system, we demonstrate the practical effectiveness and robustness of our approach. Our research contributes significantly to the domain by providing a systematic, adaptive, and scalable solution for real-time cyber security threat assessment, enhancing the resilience and security posture of critical CPS infrastructure against emerging threats.

[R0639] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Conference on Secure and Trustworthy Machine Learning (SaTML) | type=CONF
TITLE: "Org-Wide, We’re Not Ready": C-Level Lessons on Securing Generative AI Systems
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Security; Tools; Printing; Interviews; Large language models; Signal detection; Surfaces; Organizations; Generative AI security; Organizational readiness; Prompt injection; Data leakage
ABSTRACT: Enterprises are adopting Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) faster than they can secure it. We report an empirical study of 20 Canadian Chief Information Security Officers (CISOs) who combined semi-structured interviews with a full-day, practitioner-led think tank. We ask (RQ1) how leaders prioritize GenAI threats, (RQ2) where organizations are prepared across the lifecycle, and (RQ3) where governance and assurance frameworks fall short. CISOs consistently rank three exposures as high-likelihood, high-impact: (1) data movement and leakage via everyday assistant use and downstream logs/backups, (2) prompt/model misuse that steers assistants, especially RAG-backed ones, outside intended retrieval scope, and (3) deepfake voice used for authority spoofing and urgent fraud. Readiness is strongest upstream (intake reviews, data classification/lineage, architectural zoning) and weakest at runtime: few teams have EDR-like telemetry for prompts, tool calls, or agent routing, so detection remains largely human-in-the-loop. Current frameworks are principle-heavy but procedure-light and insufficiently sector-tuned. We translate these observations into actionable controls: minimum AI-EDR telemetry, sector-ready governance runbooks, and a red-teaming program that moves from single-prompt tests to end-to-end exercises spanning data→model→tools/APIs. Our findings align investment and policy with the blast-radius CISOs face today, and provide a pragmatic path from static compliance to operational assurance.

[R0640] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Vehicular Technology | type=JOUR
TITLE: A Vision-Based Covert Attack and Hybrid Adversary Detection for Autonomous Vehicles Using Generative Adversarial Network
KEYWORDS: Sensors; Wheels; Roads; Global Positioning System; Cameras; Generative adversarial networks; Cyberattack; Predictive models; Decision making; Artificial neural networks; Autonomous vehicle; covert attack; lane keeping; generative artificial intelligence; diffusion model; navigation system
ABSTRACT: Autonomous vehicles (AVs) are advancing fast and are vulnerable to covert, intelligent cyber-attacks. This paper introduces a novel covert attack and detection method for a vision-based navigation system of multi-agent AVs. The proposed method is called VCA-HADS, which consists of a vision-based covert attack (VCA) and a hybrid adversary detection system (HADS), which can be applied to a driver assistance system (DAS) of AVs. The VCA would drive the AV out of the lane. Keeping deviation effects hidden, VCA employs a generative adversarial network to manipulate the vision sensor's output such that the AV is positioned on and aligned with the road's center line. To detect the malicious behavior of AVs, a HADS is developed using a customized deep neural network, GPS data, and a road map. The DAS is also equipped with a decision-making algorithm to investigate the possibility of a VCA and to warn the driver in high-risk driving situations. To evaluate the performance of VCA-HADS in a multi-agent lane-keeping mission, various 3D driving simulations are performed. Based on the simulation results, the proposed covert attack and detection mechanism are valid and effective.

[R0641] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Innovations in Emerging Technologies for Sustainable Development (ICIETSD) | type=CONF
TITLE: YOLO-Driven UAV Surveillance System for Autonomous Threat Detection
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Weapons; Printing; Surveillance; Training; Security; Signal detection; Timing; Real-time systems; Monitoring; -UAV surveillance; drone monitoring; weapon detection; bomb detection; image analysis; deep learning methods; neural network models; computer vision; public safety monitoring
ABSTRACT: Security monitoring has become more important in recent years because weapons and explosive devices can be misused in public spaces. Places like airports, train stations, and large events need constant observation to prevent potential dangers. In many cases, security staff watch surveillance cameras, but continuous manual monitoring can be difficult and might lead to missed incidents. Therefore, researchers are looking into automated systems to assist human operators. This work presents a detection system that identifies bombs and weapons in surveillance images and drone footage. The MobileNetV2 deep learning architecture, employing transfer learning, serves as the foundation for the model. Prior to the training phase, the acquired images were subjected to resizing, normalization, and data augmentation techniques to enhance the model's learning capacity. Subsequent to training, the system's efficacy is assessed through a range of performance metrics, including accuracy, precision, recall, and ROC analysis. The findings indicate the model's capacity to accurately identify security threats while differentiating them from benign objects. Consequently, the system offers valuable support to security personnel by automating image analysis and flagging potentially suspicious items. This approach has the potential to accelerate monitoring processes and improve safety protocols in surveillance environments.

[R0642] year=2025 | venue=Chinese Journal of Electronics | type=JOUR
TITLE: RuleMaster+: LLM-Based Automated Rule Generation Framework for Intrusion Detection Systems
KEYWORDS: Training; Large language models; Intrusion detection; Training data; Manuals; Writing; Network security; Vectors; Robustness; Real-time systems; Large language model; Intrusion detection; Rule generation; Cybersecurity
ABSTRACT: Intrusion detection is vital for network security, but traditional intrusion dectetion system (IDS) face challenges in complex rule writing, requiring significant resources. The rise of large language models (LLMs) offers new possibilities, yet limited research exists on applying LLMs to IDS rule generation. Existing LLMs often fall short for this task. This paper presents RuleMaster, an LLM fine-tuned with a curated instruction dataset to automate IDS rule generation. Even for new attacks, as long as the proof-of-concept (PoC) for the attack is available, the RuleMaser can generate effective IDS rules. Our evaluation shows that RuleMaster outperforms ChatGPT in rule generation tasks, as demonstrated through win rate evaluation and manual assessment. Furthermore, we refine RuleMaster into Rule'Master-l-, which not only generates IDS rules but also provides explanations for rules and PoC, along with corresponding defense recommendations. Our approach effectively improves the quality and efficiency of rule writing, providing a new solution for enhancing IDS performance.

[R0643] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Dependable and Secure Computing | type=JOUR
TITLE: ProteusFuzz: From Static Harness Generation to Adaptive, Agent-Guided Library Fuzzing
KEYWORDS: Fuzzing; Libraries; Large language models; Application programming interfaces; Codes; Seeds (agriculture); Modeling; Computer bugs; Logic; Conferences; Library Fuzzing; Fuzzing Harness Generation; Fuzzing Seed Generation; Agent; Large Language Model
ABSTRACT: The efficacy of coverage-guided library fuzzing is strictly bound by the quality of harnesses that map unstructured inputs to semantically valid API sequences. While recent Large Language Model (LLM)-based approaches have reduced the manual burden of harness construction, they frequently suffer from a lack of domain context, yielding harnesses that fail to compile or violate implicit usage constraints. Our preliminary work, PromeFuzz (ACM CCS 2025), addressed this challenge by injecting statically extracted metadata into LLM prompts to ensure initial syntactic and semantic correctness. However, longitudinal analysis reveals a critical coverage plateau: statistical improvements in API coverage do not translate proportionally to branch coverage due to deep-seated Fuzzer Blockers, such as complex input predicates and state-dependent execution prerequisites. To transcend this limitation, we present ProteusFuzz, an adaptive, closed-loop framework that synergizes static knowledge-driven synthesis with dynamic, diagnosis-driven remediation. We propose a novel empirical taxonomy of blockers, distinguishing between seed-related barriers (requiring format-compliant inputs) and harness-related barriers (requiring state-aware driver logic). Consequently, ProteusFuzz employs a dual-path remediation strategy, leveraging an intelligent agent to orchestrate predicate-aware seed synthesis and automated harness refinement. Extensive evaluation on 20 widely used C/C++ libraries demonstrates that ProteusFuzz achieves average branch coverage improvements of 16.18% over PromeFuzz, 27.24% over PromptFuzz, and 16.80% over CKGFuzzer, while further outperforming OSS-Fuzz-Gen and OSS-Fuzz by 43.12% and 32.10%, respectively. Furthermore, it uncovered 21 previously unknown bugs, including 15 confirmed or fixed bugs, 2 of which have been assigned CVE IDs. These results underscore that bridging static correctness with dynamic adaptability is essential for advancing the autonomy and depth of library fuzzing.

[R0644] year=2026 | venue=2026 Technology and Innovation Conference (TIC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Machine Learning for Modern Cybersecurity: Trend-Driven Architectures, Threat Models, and Quantitative Evaluation
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Security; Labeling; Sequences; Sequential analysis; Signal detection; Retrieval augmented generation; Toxicology; Training; Transformers; Cybersecurity; machine learning; adversarial ML; intrusion detection; malware detection; prompt injection; retrieval poisoning; robust learning; transformer models
ABSTRACT: Machine learning (ML) has become a default substrate for contemporary cybersecurity—from network intrusion detection and malware classification to phishing defense and security analytics over cloud telemetry. At the same time, cybersecurity is now a first-class stress test for ML: adversaries actively manipulate inputs, labels, data pipelines, and even model supply chains. Recent trends amplify both promise and risk: transformer backbones that learn directly from raw traffic or byte streams; self-supervised pretraining over petabyte-scale logs; federated and privacy-preserving learning at the network edge; and agentic large language model (LLM) copilots that triage incidents and generate remediation steps. This paper presents a complete, quantitative research study of ML-for-cybersecurity under these trends. We propose a threat-aware end-to-end pipeline, GuardML, that integrates (i) representation learning for heterogeneous security signals, (ii) robustness layers against evasion/poisoning/extraction, and (iii) operational guardrails for deployment (calibration, abstention, provenance-aware retrieval for RAG). We develop reproducible experimental methodology and stage-wise energy/latency models, then evaluate on a curated multi-domain benchmark protocol (TRISHIELD) spanning network intrusion, malware, and phishing. Across tasks, a trendaligned transformer stack achieves macro-F1 scores of 0.91 for NIDS, 0.94 for malware, and 0.95 for phishing, representing improvements of 4–11 points over gradient-boosted decision tree baselines. Under adversarial evasion, our guardrail-enhanced models maintain true positive rates of 0.91 at 0.1% false positive rate for NIDS compared to 0.71 for undefended baselines. We show that lightweight, composable guardrails reduce attack success rates by 32–61% with only 7–9% latency overhead, and we derive five actionable design rules linking model choice, feature exposure, and security posture.


